COMMIT 99d8d046c194b000773425a676b5055ace812ca1
docs(core,runtime): record why the eight narrow catches are right

F-30's sweep measured all 165 catch clauses in src/main and left ten it
could not judge without answering one question each. The triage settled
them earlier today and D-113 holds the verdicts: eight RIGHT, two WRONG,
the two WRONG split out as FIX-149 and FIX-150. This closes the row by
giving each RIGHT clause one line of reason, so the next sweep reads it
instead of re-deriving it.

Comments only. 8 insertions and 7 deletions, the deletions being the
seven lines that had text appended. No executable line changed, so no
mutants and no coverage are owed -- a scoped PIT run on these three
classes would be identical to main's and claiming one would be noise.

The eight fall in three groups. Two are contained one frame out by a
deliberately wider clause, and that claim was settled by grepping each
method's callers across the tree rather than by proximity in the file:
rebuild has exactly one caller, inside rollBack's try, so the
RuntimeException-or-Error clause owns it and install's Throwable clause
never sees it; consumers has exactly two, on paths with different
clauses. Four are policy, where a problem line for an Error would lie
about the flow, and each comment states both halves, because the deploy
path contains an Error while the validate path -- whose try catches only
DeployRefusedException -- lets it leave. Two are narrow on purpose with
the javadoc already saying so, and there the comment points at the
sentence that exists.

The reviewer verified every claim independently and checked D-113's own
text for the failure mode that cost FIX-145 three rounds: a rule
attributed to a decision that does not contain it. There is none.

Its one fix before this commit was earned. AdminServer's comment said
"run()'s Throwable clause", but inside ran the identifier run is a
Supplier<Envelope> parameter whose get() is called on the line above, so
the reference resolved to the wrong thing in the one place it must not.
It now names the method by signature, run(Route, RoutingContext), which
a Supplier can never be mistaken for.

Also recorded in agent/NOTES.md, found by the implementer before this
commit: a lane's directory name is not its branch name. The worktree at
flottila-lane2 is on branch lane5, and branches lane2 and lane4 are
checked out nowhere. Both orphans were checked and carry nothing unique
-- lane2's f318124 has the same patch-id as its integrated twin 4cbc121
-- but a reset aimed at an assumed branch name could have discarded real
work. Packets name the path from now on.

